                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I pre-ordered Victoria 3 with so much excitement, but I honestly still find myself going back to play Victoria 2 instead. Just like every other recent Paradox release, this game launched as an empty, soulless shell. It feels like it’s going to take years and at least 20 more overpriced DLCs—which I am expected to pay for one by one—just to make it a fully playable and complete experience. I am so tired, man. I am completely exhausted by this endless cycle of paying full price for unfinished games and waiting years for them to be fixed. I won't be playing this anymore.\n\nDisastrous Warfare System: They completely removed player agency. The new frontline system is a buggy, frustrating mess. Instead of strategic maneuvering, you just assign a general and watch a UI progress bar go back and forth while the AI makes terrible tactical decisions.\n\nGlorified Construction Simulator: The economic system, which should be the heart of a Victoria game, just boils down to staring at a construction queue. You spend hours simply clicking to build more logging camps and iron mines while waiting for the bar to fill up.\n\nZero Historical Flavor: Every single country plays exactly the same. Whether you are playing in Europe, Asia, or South America, there is no unique flavor, local mechanics, or soul to the nations.\n\nUnbearable Late-Game Lag: As is tradition with Paradox titles, the engine completely chokes as the timeline progresses. By the late 1800s, the performance issues and lag make it an absolute chore to even reach the end date.\n\nI am done waiting for Paradox to finish their games post-launch. Save your money and just stick to Victoria 2.",
